  <h2>What "preferred vendor" means</h2>
  <p>Many insurers run programs with restoration companies that agree to their rules: response times, pricing methods, photo and moisture documentation, and how estimates are submitted. When the adjuster offers to "send someone", that's usually a company from this network.</p>
  <p>It isn't a scam, and it isn't a favor. It's a business arrangement that can genuinely make your claim easier, with one built-in tension: the company is part of a program set up by the party paying the bill.</p>

  <h2>The honest pros and cons</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Insurer's preferred vendor</th><th>Your own company</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Speed of approval</strong></td><td>Often faster: pricing and paperwork are pre-agreed</td><td>Can be slower if the insurer questions prices or scope</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Getting someone out today</strong></td><td>Often good: the insurer can dispatch</td><td>Depends on local demand; a local company may be quicker</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Who they answer to</strong></td><td>You, and the insurer's program rules</td><td>You</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Price disputes</strong></td><td>Fewer, because prices are agreed</td><td>More likely; good documentation settles most</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Choice</strong></td><td>Whoever is assigned</td><td>You can check reviews, certifications and references first</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Guarantee</strong></td><td>Ask whether the program adds one</td><td>The company's own warranty</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Do you have to use the insurer's company?</h2>
  <p>In most cases, no. Your policy is a promise to pay for covered, reasonable work, not to pick your contractor. But rules and policy wording vary, so do two things before you decide:</p>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Ask the insurer directly:</strong> "If I use my own restoration company, does anything change about approval, payment or guarantees?" Write down the answer and who gave it.</li>
    <li><strong>Check your state insurance department</strong> if anyone tells you that you <em>must</em> use a particular company. Each state's department has a consumer line; the NAIC lists them all.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Seven questions for whichever company you choose</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Are your technicians IICRC certified</strong> for water damage restoration? (The IICRC S500 standard is the industry's drying standard.)</li>
    <li><strong>Will you take and share daily moisture readings and photos?</strong></li>
    <li><strong>What will you remove, and why?</strong> Ask them to explain any tear-out before they do it, unless it's an emergency.</li>
    <li><strong>How do you price the job?</strong> Many companies estimate with the same software insurers use; ask for an itemized estimate.</li>
    <li><strong>Do you bill my insurer directly, and what do I owe if part isn't covered?</strong></li>
    <li><strong>What am I signing?</strong> A work authorization is normal. An assignment of benefits is a bigger decision; see <a href="/guides/restoration-work-authorization-assignment-of-benefits/">before you sign</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Who guarantees the work, and for how long?</strong></li>
  </ol>

  <h2>A simple way to decide</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Choose the insurer's vendor</strong> if speed matters most, you don't have a company you trust, and the insurer confirms the work is guaranteed.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose your own company</strong> if you have one you trust, the vendor can't come quickly, or you want to vet certifications and reviews yourself. Expect to keep documentation tidy so the bill is easy to approve.</li>
    <li><strong>Either way,</strong> photograph the damage before work starts, keep copies of all moisture logs, and don't sign anything you haven't read.</li>
  </ul>

